## 1. Introduction
The Fenton process has been attracted the interests of plenty of researchers and extensively used in environmental remediation by catalyzing the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ into toxic hydroxyl radicals (OH) to further initiate the degradation of organic pollutants [1–3]. In most traditional Fenton/Fentonlike process, the slow efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe(\overline{{II}})/F e(\overline{{I I I}})}$ cycle was the main limitation of degradation efficiency [4]. Then, light energy was introduced into Fenton/Fenton-like systems to promote the transformation of $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})/F e(\mathrm{III})}$ cycle and avoid secondary pollution [5– 8]. Simultaneously, heterogeneous photo-Fenton systems were developed to avoid the generation of iron sludge created in many homogeneous Fenton process [8–10]. For instance, Pan et al. reported an UV/pre-magnetized $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}/\mathrm{H}_{2}{\ O}_{2}$ process $(\mathrm{UV}/\mathrm{pre}-\mathrm{Fe}^{0}/$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\cdot$ ) for sulfamethazine (SMT) removal, in which the removal efficiency was enhanced 1.8 times by introducing UV irradiation [11]. Besides, sludge derived carbon (SC) [12] and pillared clays (PILCs) [13] based photo-Fenton systems were also applied for ciprofloxacin degradation and treatment of winery wastewater, respectively. However, the utilization of UV irradiation in these UV-driven photo-Fenton systems required specific equipment and made the degradation process more complicated and costly. Therefore, developing heterogeneous photo-Fenton systems driven by visible-light for the elimination of organic pollutants are considerably attractive.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of metal–organic hybrid materials consisting by organic linkers and inorganic metal nodes (ions, chains, or clusters) [14]. Due to their features of modularity, adjustable pore structure, open metal sites, stability and low cost, some MOFs-based photo-Fenton systems have been explored [15,16]. For example, Gao et al. reported the MIL-53 ( $\mathrm{Fe)/H_{2}O_{2}/v i s}$ system for removing two typical pharmaceuticals (clofibric acid and carbamazepine) from water [17]. In addition, MOF-based heterojunctions photo-Fenton systems also were developed to improve the photodegradation efficiency. Due to the interfacial synergistic effect between MIL-100(Fe) and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , the photo-Fenton catalysis efficiencies of MIL-100 (Fe) composites were greatly enhanced [18–20]. The photocatalytic degradation of bifunctional ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) composites towards methylene blue reached nearly $100\%$ , which was attributed to not only the improved adsorption performance of MOFs, but also the formed heterostructure between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MOFs which enhanced the photogenerated charge transfer [21]. However, these reported strategies usually required complicated synthesis procedures, making the preparation of photo-Fenton catalysts tedious and time-consuming. Therefore, developing MOFs-based photoFenton catalysts with high oxidation capacity and simple preparation process is meaningful.
Benefiting from the modularity and synthetic tunability of MOFs, it was feasible to design their building blocks for further programing the features of MOFs, such as morphologies, sizes, porosity and chemical properties [22]. Herein, four rod-shaped Fe-MOFs (namely Fe-TCPP-1, Fe-TCPP-2, Fe-TCPP-3, Fe-TCPP-4) with the increasing aspect ratios were rapidly synthesized with the assistant of microwave, using meso-Tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl) porphyrin $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T C P P})$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions and benzoic acid (BA) as organic linkers, metal nodes and regulators, respectively (Scheme 1A). Among them, Fe-TCPP-3 exhibited the largest pore structure and good photochemical activity. With visible-light irradiation, the photogenerated electrons $(e^{-})$ of TCPP ligands in FeTCPP-3 could transfer to the metal nodes Fe-O clusters by intermolecular electron transfer to in-situ reduce $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , to further accelerate the transformation of $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})/F e(\mathrm{III})}$ cycle. The insitu formed $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ further catalyzed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposed into strong oxidizing reactive oxygen species (ROS) to further oxidize substrates into small molecules (Scheme 1B). Furthermore, the FeTCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system was applied to efficiently degrade ciprofloxacin (CIP) under neutral condition, attributing to the high production of holes $(h^{+}),e^{-}$ , OH, superoxide anion radicals $(0_{2}^{-})$ and singlet oxygen $(^{1}0_{2})$ , which collectively oxidized CIP into small molecules. This study provided a perspective to obtain MOFs-based photo-catalysts with high photo-Fenton activity by rationally designing the building blocks and assistant ligands of MOFs.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and Reagents
The organic ligand meso-Tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin ${\mathrm{.H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ , $97\%$ purity) was purchased from Annoron Biotechnology Co., ltd (Beijing, China). Ferric chloride $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $99\%$ purity) and benzoic acid (BA, $99\%$ purity) were supplied by Macklin Biotechnology Co., ltd (Shanghai, China) and Wuhan Organic Synthesis Chemical Factory (Wuhan, China), respectively. N, N’- dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\ \%$ purity), triethanolamine (TEOA, $99\%$ purity), potassium dichromate $\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}$ · $99\%$ purity), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $99\%$ purity) and hydrogen peroxide $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2},30\%)$ were obtained from Chuandong Chemical Co., ltd (Chongqing, China). Ciprofloxacin hydrochloride (CIP, $98\%$ purity) and 3, 30, 5, $5^{\prime}$ -tetramethylbenzidine dihydrochloride (TMB, $98\%$ purity) were purchased from Aladdin Reagents Co., ltd (Shanghai, China). $p.$ Benzoquinone $(\mathsf{B Q},\mathsf{\Omega}9\mathsf{\Omega}\%$ purity) was obtained from Huaxia Reagents Co., ltd (Chengdu, China). All used reagents were analytical purity without further purification before use. The deionized water $(18.2\mathrm{~m~}\Omega)$ was used in the whole study.
## 2.2. Apparatus
The household microwave oven (M1-L213B, Midea Co., ltd., China) was used to prepare Fe-MOFs. The morphology and size of Fe-MOFs were conducted by an S-4800 scanning electron microscope (SEM) (Hitachi, Japan) and a Tecnai G2 F30 field-emission transmission electron microscope (TEM) (FEI, USA). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of Fe-MOFs were characterized by D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer (Bruker, Germany), using Cu Ka radiation $(\lambda\:=\:1.5406\:\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ). The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and thermogravimetry analysis (TGA) of Fe-MOFs were determined by ESCALAB 250Xi (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) and STA 449 F5/F3 Jupiter thermogravimetric Analyzer (NETZSCH, Germany), respectively. The A300-10/12 electron spin resonance (ESR) spectrometer (Bruker, Germany) was used to record the ESR spectra at room temperature. The UV–vis spectra were determined by U-3010 spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan). The infrared spectra of Fe-MOFs were measured by an IRPrestige-21 Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FTIR, Shimazu, Japan) using the KBr pellet technique. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and Mott-Schottky plot measurements were performed on a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation (Shanghai, China) in a three-electrode system with a saturated $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ , Pt wire and modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as reference electrode, counter electrode and working electrode, respectively. The photocurrenttime response curves (i-t curves) was determined by an electrochemical workstation with 300 W Xe lamp equipping with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter (CEL-PECX2000) (Beijing, China). The electrospray ionization mass spectrometry (ESI-MS) of CIP was determined by an IMPACT II high resolution time-of-flight mass spectrometer (Bruker, Germany).
## 2.3. Synthesis of Fe-MOFs nanorods
The preparation process of four Fe-MOFs with different aspect ratios was as follows: Firstly, organic ligand ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf T}{\sf C}{\sf P}{\sf P}$ $20.0\ \mathrm{mg},$ $0.025\mathrm{\mmol};$ ) and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $20.3~\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.075 mmol) were dissolved in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, respectively. BA $(732.7~\mathrm{mg}$ , $6.0\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) was dissolved in $6~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. Then $400~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ , $400~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and a certain volume of BA $0~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ for Fe-TCPP-1, $25~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ for FeTCPP-2, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ for Fe-TCPP-3, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ for Fe-TCPP-4) were added into a $3~\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex vial, and the mixture was diluted to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ by DMF. The capped vials (six vials for one batch) were evenly placed into a household microwave oven about $3\mathrm{cm}$ away from the center of its rotating plate. Under the microwave irradiation, the mixture was heated for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ with medium–low power mode (about 231 W). After reaction, the mixture was cooled down to room temperature and centrifuged at 13500 rpm for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . After that, the resulting Fe-MOFs nanorods were respectively washed by fresh DMF and ethanol three times, which were further redispersed in ethanol and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
Determination of the concentration of Fe-MOFs: Firstly, the empty EP tubes were weighted by an electronic balance as ${\mathfrak{m}}_{0}$ , and a certain volume of Fe-MOFs ethanol dispersion solution $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathbf{x}})$ was added into the tube. Then, the solution was centrifugated at 13000 rpm for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ and collected the precipitation which was dried at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the tubes were weighted again as $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathrm{x}}$ . The concentration of Fe-MOFs was determined by the following equation:
$$
c_{F e-M O F s}={\frac{\mathrm{m_{x}-m_{0}}}{\mathrm{V_{x}}}}\ (\mathrm{mg/mL})
$$
## 2.4. Photocurrent measurement
Firstly, the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was polished with $0.05~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}~\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ powder for $3\ \mathrm{min}$ , then washed by ethanol and deionized water respectively, and dried by ${\sf N}_{2}$ . Then, $7.0~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ FeMOFs dispersion $(0.2\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ with different aspect ratios was dropped onto GCE and naturally dried in the air. For the photoelectrochemical (PEC) performance test: The i-t curves of Fe-MOFs/GCE in $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ deoxygenated ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ solution were measured by using CEL-PECX2000 photoelectrochemical workstation with 300 W Xe lamp irradiation equipping with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter.
## 2.5. Electrochemical measurement
For a typical electrochemical impedance measurement, after polishing and modifying the GCE, the electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) of Fe-MOFs/GCE was measured in $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ KCl solution (containing $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L\K_{4}[F e(C N)_{6}]}$ and $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L\K_{3}/F e(C N)_{6}])}$ with a $10~\mathrm{kHz}$ frequency under stable open circuit voltage.
Mott-Schottky plot measurement: All working GCE was polished, washed and dried before use. After that, $7.0~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Fe-TCPP-3 dispersion $(0.2~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ was used to modify the GCE and dried in the air. Then, Mott-Schottky plots of Fe-TCPP-3/GCE under different frequencies $(1000\ \mathrm{Hz},\ 1500\ \mathrm{Hz},\ 2000\ \mathrm{Hz})$ were measured in $0.1\mathrm{mol/L}$ deoxygenated ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ solution by CHI 660E electrochemical workstation.
## 2.6. Determination of photo-Fenton property of Fe-TCPP-3
The TMB- $\cdot{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ system was used to investigate the photoFenton activity of Fe-TCPP-3. Typically, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Fe-TCPP-3 dispersion solution $(0.2\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ , $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution $(10.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ , and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ TMB solution $(10.0\ \mathrm{\mmol/L})$ were added into a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ EP tube, and then diluted to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ with ultrapure water. The mixture solution was exposed to a 300 W Xe lamp (CELHXF300, China Education Au-light Co., ltd, Beijing) equipped with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter under the current as 15 A for $10\mathrm{min}$ , while the unilluminated groups were placed in the dark for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . After reaction, the solution was centrifuged at 14500 rpm for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the absorption spectra of the obtained supernatant in the range of $500{-}750~\mathrm{~nm}$ were measured on a U-3010 spectrophotometer.
## 2.7. Determination of CIP degradation curves
For a typical procedure of CIP photodegradation, the total volume of degradation reaction was $20.0~\mathrm{{mL}}$ and the whole reaction was carried out at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ . Firstly, $1.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ Fe-TCPP-3 dispersion $(0.2\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ , $1.0\ \mathrm{\mL}$ CIP solution $(1.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ and $15.6\ \mathrm{\mL}$ ultrapure water were added into a glass bottle. The mixture solution was stirred in the dark for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ for pre-adsorption. Then, $1.4~\mathrm{mL}$ NaOH solution $(1.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ used for adjusting to neutral pH and 1.0 mL $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution $(10.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ were added into the bottle. The mixture was immediately exposed to a 300 W Xe lamp equipped with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter with the distance of $11\ \mathrm{{\cm}}$ . During the photo-Fenton degradation process, 1 mL reaction solution was taken out at a certain illuminated time (0, 5, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 min) and centrifuged at $14500\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\ \mathrm{\min}$ . The absorption spectra of obtained supernatant in the range of $220\mathrm{-}382~\mathrm{nm}$ were determined by a U-3010 spectrophotometer.
## 2.8. Inhibition experiment
The specific procedures were as follows: $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Fe-TCPP-3 dispersion $(0.2~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ CIP solution $\left(1.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L}\right)$ and $730~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ultrapure water were added into a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ EP tube successively. Then the mixture was placed in a constant temperature mixer (ThermoMixer C, Eppendorf, Germany) at $400\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ for pre-adsorption. After that, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2}$ solution $\mathrm{(10.0~mmol/}$ L), $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ inhibitor solution $\left(1.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L}\right)$ and $70~\mathrm{\upmuL}~1.0~\mathrm{mmol/L}$ NaOH solution were added into the above mixture. In the end, the tube was illuminated by a 300 W Xe lamp equipped with a $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ cut-off filter for $30~\mathrm{min}$ After reaction, the mixture was centrifuged at $14500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the absorption spectra $(220-382~\mathrm{{\nm}})$ of supernatant were recorded on a U-3010 spectrophotometer.
## 2.9. Effect of dissolved oxygen
Similar to the procedure of inhibition experiment, the used solution was prepared by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -saturated, Air-saturated and $0_{2}$ - saturated ultrapure water, respectively. Typically, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Fe-TCPP3 dispersion $(0.2\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ , $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ CIP solution $(1.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ and $780~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ultrapure water were added into a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ EP tube. Then the mixture was placed in a constant temperature mixer (ThermoMixer C, Eppendorf, Germany) at $400\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ for preadsorption. After that, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2}$ solution $(10.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ and $70~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}~1.0~\mathrm{mmol/L}~\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution were added into the above mixture. In the end, the tube was illuminated by a 300 W Xe lamp equipped with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . After reaction, the mixture was centrifuged at 14500 rpm for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the absorption spectra $(220-382~\mathrm{nm})$ of supernatant were recorded on a U-3010 spectrophotometer.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Fe-MOFs nanorods
Using ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ as organic ligand and metal nodes, four Fe-MOFs owning different length-diameter ratios were rapidly synthesized with the assistance of microwave and the additional different concentrations of BA. As shown in SEM and TEM images, the size of four rod-shaped Fe-MOFs decreased with the increase BA concentrations, and their length-diameter ratios were decreased (Fig. 1 and Fig.S1). In order to explore the bonding between organic ligands with metal ions in Fe-MOFs, the FTIR spectra were measured (Fig. S2). The peaks at $1697~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1604~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the asymmetrical stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ . The peak at $1697\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifted to $1654\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the peak at $1604~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ shifted to $1527~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In addition, the peak at $1265\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigning to stretching vibration of $c{-}0$ in ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ disappeared after forming Fe-MOFs. These results indicated that $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ was coordinated to the carboxyl groups of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ to form FeMOFs [23].
The XRD patterns of the as-prepared four Fe-MOFs were measured, demonstrating the same crystal structure of these four MOFs which were similar to the peaks of PCN-222 (CDCC number: 893545) (Fig. 2A). Moreover, taking Fe-TCPP-3 as example, the $X-$ ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out to further confirm the bonding of metal ions and organic ligands, which indicated the existence of four elements (C, N, O, Fe) in Fe-MOFs (Fig. 2B). In the high-resolution XPS spectra of C 1 s, the peaks at 284.8, 285.1 and $288.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ belonged to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ , $C{\mathrm{-}}C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ and $0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0$ bond, respectively (Fig. S3A) [24]. The signals at $531.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $533.6\mathrm{eV}$ in the high-resolution XPS spectra of O 1 s were assigned to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}0{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ bond in as-prepared Fe-TCPP-3, respectively (Fig. 2C) [25]. Additionally, the Fe 2p high-resolution XPS spectra of Fe-TCPP-3 showed two main peaks at 711.7 eV and $725.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ which were assigned to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. The peaks at $711.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $723.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , and the peaks at $713.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 725.3 were pertained to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , along with two fingerprint peaks at $718.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $733.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ [26,27], indicating that iron nodes in Fe-TCPP-3 were multivalent states and successful coordinated with the carbonyl groups in ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ (Fig. 2D). The high-resolution N 1 s XPS spectra confirmed that $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions were not bonding into the center of porphyrin ring, which further testified by the existence of four $\mathsf{Q}$ -band absorption in UV spectra (Fig. S3B and Fig. S4) [25].
Furthermore, the results of ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and the pore size distribution curves of the four Fe-MOFs nanorods with different length-diameter ratios indicated their high BET surface area and porosity (Fig. S5). The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms curves of four Fe-MOFs nanorods showed the hysteresis loop in the high relative pressure range, which could be classified as type IV and suggested the mesoporous structure in four FeMOFs [28,29]. Except for Fe-TCPP-1, the pore distribution of other three Fe-MOFs (Fe-TCPP-2, Fe-TCPP-3, Fe-TCPP-4) was similar and had a wide pore size distribution containing both micropores and mesopores, proving their hierarchical pore structures (Table S1) [30]. Besides, four Fe-MOFs nanorods with different lengthdiameter ratios exhibited similar thermogravimetric analysis curves (Fig. S6). The weight loss of Fe-MOFs in the range of $20-$ $380^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ was mainly corresponding to the solvent molecules, such as the evaporation of adsorbed molecules ( $_\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ethanol) and the decomposition of coordinated DMF molecules [4,21]. With the temperature raised, the ligand TCPP in the MOFs was decomposed and the framework structure was collapsed. These results indicated that Fe-MOFs nanorods exhibited superior thermal stability at about $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## 3.2. Photo-Fenton activity of Fe-MOFs
The porphyrin molecules are widely used in the fields of photocatalysis and photodynamic therapy [31]. Thus, the photoelectrochemical (PEC) properties of four Fe-MOFs nanorods with different length-diameter ratios was firstly investigated to unveil their $e^{-}{-}h^{+}$ separation efficiency. As shown in Fig. 3A, the FeTCPP-3 exhibited the highest photocurrent comparing to other three Fe-MOFs (Fe-TCPP-1, Fe-TCPP-2, Fe-TCPP-4), indicating its highest PEC performance. In addition, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was studied to prove the charge transfer of Fe-TCPP-3 in the PEC process. It was observed that the resistance value of Fe-TCPP-3 was distinctly lower than that of other three FeMOFs nanorods, indicating the better conductivity and higher charge transfer rate in Fe-TCPP-3 (Fig. 3B).
Moreover, the Soret band of Fe-TCPP-3 ( $422\ \mathrm{nm}$ showed the largest red shift contrasting with ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ ligand $(416~\mathrm{nm})$ and the widest half-peak width, testifying its best light capture capability with comparison to other three Fe-MOFs (Fig. 3C and Fig. S4) [32]. All these results collectively suggested that the Fe-TCPP-3 exhibited the highest $e^{-}{-}h^{+}$ separation efficiency and best light capture ability among these four Fe-MOFs nanorods. Furthermore, the TMB- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system was applied to evaluate its photo-Fenton activity. The absorbance of $\mathrm{TMB+H}_{2}{\cal O}_{2}{\cdot}+\mathrm{Fe}$ -TCPP-3-light group was remarkably enhanced by contrast to control groups, which was 3.2-, 5.1- and 59.0-fold to the absorbance of $\mathrm{TMB}{+}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}{+}\mathrm{Fe}{-}$ TCPP-3-dark, TMB+Fe-TCPP-3-light and TMB+Fe-TCPP-3-dark groups, respectively (Fig. 3D). Thus, Fe-TCPP-3 exhibited superior photo-Fenton property, which was probably attributed to its hierarchical and biggest pore structure such as highest pore volume and biggest average pore size (Table S1). The biggest pore structure of Fe-TCPP-3 would allow more substrate molecules get into and be accessible to the active catalytic sites in Fe-TCPP-3.
## 3.3. Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system for CIP degradation
By reason of the abuse of antibiotics, the water pollution caused by antibitic residuals demands to be solved in green and efficient approach [33–35]. As one of the commonly used antibiotics, fluoroquinolones (FQs) have been found as one of the residuals in the water bodies and would cause the generation of FQ-resistent bacteria, possessing potential hazardous effects on humans and other organisms [36]. Taking ciprofloxacin (CIP) as an example, the FeTCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system for CIP degradation was explored. With the increase of light irradiation time, the absorbance of CIP gradually decreased and reached the degradation platform at $30\mathrm{min}$ in Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system (Fig. 4A). However, the absorbance of $\mathrm{CIP+H}_{2}0_{2}{+}\mathrm{I}$ e-TCPP-3 without light irradiation slightly decreased (Fig. S7), suggested the high photoFenton activity of Fe-TCPP-3 for CIP degradation. Then, the degradation curves with various concentration of Fe-TCPP-3 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were tested (Fig. 4B and Fig. S8). With the increased concentration of reactants, the CIP degradation rate gradually increased and reached steady state. Besides, it was shown that only the photoFenton system exhibited high CIP degradation efficiency $(\sim73\ \%)$ , while other systems just leaded slight CIP degradation (Fig. 4C).
To intuitively compare the degradation efficiency, the CIP degradation kinetic curves of different systems based on pseudofirst-order model $(-\mathrm{ln}C/C_{0}=k_{a b s}\mathrm{t})$ were calculated, where $C_{0}$ , C and $k$ represents the initial concentration of CIP, the CIP concentration at the time interval t, and reaction rate constant, respectively. The disappearance rate constant $(k_{a b s})$ which revealed the degradation efficiency were listed (Fig. 4D). The $k_{a b s}$ of Fe-TCPP-3-based photo-Fenton system was about 19-, 26- and 218-fold towards the photodynamic- ( ${\mathrm{CIP+Fe}}$ -TCPP-3-light), peroxidase- (CIP $+H_{2}O_{2}+F e$ -TCPP-3-dark) and oxidase-based (CIP+Fe-TCPP-3-dark) systems, respectively. In the meantime, the degradation efficiency of Fe-TCPP-3-based photo-Fenton system just slightly decreased after four recycles and the SEM images of Fe-TCPP-3 after reaction also showed negligible morphology change, indicating the high stability and activity of Fe-TCPP-3 as catalyst in photo-Fenton reaction (Fig. S9 and S10). Besides, the IR spectra of Fe-TCPP-3 demonstrated that the function groups in Fe-TCPP-3 were not changed after photo-Fenton reaction (Fig S11). Additionally, comparing to the small molecular size of CIP (Fig. S12), the large pore structure endowed Fe-TCPP-3 as a microreactor with the capability to increase the concentration of CIP in the local microenvironment, thus remarkably enhanced the CIP degradation efficiency. All these results demonstrated that the superior photo-Fenton activity and stability of Fe-TCPP-3 could effectively catalyze the CIP degradation.
The comparison of degradation efficiency between Fe-TCPP-3 and other reported studies were listed in Table S2. Comparing to the large using amount of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ ion $(50~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ in homogeneous photo-Fenton system, the low using dosage of heterogeneous catalysts showed excellent catalytic activity and application perspective [37]. Besides, the value of the $k_{a b s}$ of $\mathrm{CIP+H_{2}O_{2}+F e}$ -TCPP-3 system was almost the highest by comparing with that of other catalysts-based photocatalytic systems, such as sludge derived carbon (SC), $_{\mathsf{A g-F e-T i O}_{2}}$ composite, $\mathsf{A g/A g B r/B i V O_{4}}$ composite, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ and FeCit [12,36,38–40]. Under neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ condition, the FeTCPP-3-based photo-Fenton system showed high degradation efficiency and short degradating time, which would show the potential to be more time- and cost-saving in practical pollution treatment.
## 3.4. Degradation mechanism of Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system
It is well known that the photo-Fenton activity comes from the generated $e^{-},h^{+}$ , reactive oxygen species (ROS) [41]. Firstly, potassium persulfate $(\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{C r}_{2}0_{7})$ , triethanolamine (TEOA), methanol (MeOH), $p$ -benzoquinone (BQ) and sodium azide $(\mathsf{N a N}_{3})$ were used as $e^{-},h^{+}$ , OH, $\ O_{2}^{-}$ and $^10_{2}$ inhibitor, respectively. According to the results of inhibition experiment, the degradation efficiencies in the presence of these five inhibitors were decreased, indicating the participation of these five reactive species in the Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system (Fig. 5A). Besides, the function of dissolved oxygen (DO) was also evaluated in this reaction. Interestingly, with the increase of the content of DO, the CIP degradation efficiency was gradually decreased, indicating that the DO could quench the photo-Fenton activity (Fig. 5B). It was probably attributed to that the DO would compete the photogenerated $e^{-}$ with $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ nodes, which hindered the reduction of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and further resulted in lower photo-Fenton activity. Moreover, the ROS generation in Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system was measured by the ESR spectra. The 1:2:2:1 characteristic ESR peaks of DMPO-OH and their higher intensities under irradiation confirmed the generation of OH in Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton reaction (Fig. 5C). Similar to OH, the formation of $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ also proved by the ESR spectra of ${\tt D M P O-O_{2}^{\cdot-}}$ and TEMP- $^10_{2}$ adducts (Fig. 5D and Fig. S13). Therefore, these three ROS were generated in the Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton reaction for CIP degradation.
## 3.5. Possible degradation pathways
To clarify the charge-carrier transfer pathway in the Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system, the band structure and the band edge of Fe-TCPP-3 were measured. As depicted in Fig. 6A, the results of Mott-Schottky plots showed that the conduction band energy $(\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}})$ of Fe-TCPP-3 was $-0.55\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . Besides, the diffuse reflectance spectrum of Fe-TCPP-3 displayed excellent absorption in the range of $400{-}700\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the energy gap $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}\right)$ was measured as $1.64\ \mathrm{eV}$ by Tauc plot method, thus the valence band (VB, $\mathrm{{E}_{V B}=\mathrm{{E}_{g}+\mathrm{{E}_{C B})}}}$ 1 energy was caculated as $1.09\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 6B and Fig. S14). The band structure of Fe-TCPP-3 was concluded in Fig. 6C accordding to the above results. By contrast to the standard potential value of
$0_{2}/0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ $\cdot-0.33\ \mathrm{eV})$ [42], the CB potential of Fe-TCPP-3 $\left(-0.55\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ was more negative, thus the photogenerated $e^{-}$ could reduce $0_{2}$ to $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ which was consistent with the results of DO influence. But the VB potential of Fe-TCPP-3 $(1.09{\mathrm{~eV}})$ was also more negative than the standard potential of $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{O}}\mathrm{H}^{\cdot}$ (2.38 eV) [42], indicating that the photogenerated $h^{+}$ could not directly oxidaze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ into OH. Therefore, the generated OH was $\cdot\cdot$ formed from the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ catalyzed by Fe-TCPP-3.
Based on all above studies, we spectaculated that the mechanism of Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system were as follow (Scheme S1): (1) Firstly, the TCPP ligands in Fe-TCPP-3 generated $e^{-}$ and $h^{+}$ with the visible-light irradiation, which could react with $0_{2}$ to form $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ (eq. 1–2). (2) Besides, the photo-induced $e^{-}$ could transfer to the adjacent Fe-O clusters to reduce $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ by intramolecular electron transfer and promoted the dynamic Fe $(\mathbb{T})/\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})$ cycle, resulting high photo-Fenton activity of Fe-TCPP3 and resisting the recombination of $e^{-}\cdot h^{+}$ pairs efficiently (eq. 3). (3) The in-situ formed $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ exhibited excellent Fenton activity to catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decompose into more ROS (OH, $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ , $^10_{2})$ (eq. $\llangle-$ 5). (4) The produced reactive species $(e^{-},h^{+},0\mathrm{H},0_{2}^{-},{}^{1}0_{2})$ could effectively and continuously oxidize CIP to degradate (eq. 6). Moreover, the mass spectra of CIP with different reaction time were measured to further verify the successful degradation of CIP (Fig. S15). With the increase of light irradiation time, the intensity of molecular ion peak of ciprofloxacin $(m/z=332)$ decreased gradually. While the molecular ion peaks in the range of low mass-tocharge ratios such as $m/z=138$ and 274 appeared and the intensity gradually increased, indicating that CIP was successfully degrade to small molecules. Combined with the reported studies, the possible pathway of degradation of CIP by Fe-TCPP-3 based photoFenton system was summarized in Fig. S16 [43,44].
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, four Fe-MOFs nanorods with different lengthdiameter ratios were synthesized by adjusting the addition of BA in a simple and fast way. Among them, the Fe-TCPP-3 exhibited high electron-hole separation efficiency and biggest pore structure, which could act as a microreactor to improve the substrate concentration in the local microenvironment to enhance the catalytic activity. With light irradiation, TCPP ligands in Fe-TCPP-3 could produce photogenerated $e^{-}$ and $h^{+}$ , and $e^{-}$ could transfer to Fe-O clusters to reduce $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and facilitated the conversion of $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})/F e(\mathrm{III})}$ cycle, resulting in superior photo-Fenton activity of Fe-TCPP-3. The in-situ formed $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ exhibited high Fenton activity to catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decompose into ROS, which could effectively oxidize substrates. Additionally, the Fe-TCPP-3 based photo-Fenton system was applied for efficiently degrading CIP to small molecules under neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ condition. In this work, the prepared Fe-MOFs also showed great potential to be used in water treatment, in which the disinfection and sterilization required neutral pH condition.
